---
import GovernanceDocument from '~/components/ui/GovernanceDocument.astro';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';

const sections = [
  { id: 'what-the-drug-war-cost', title: 'What the drug war cost' },
  { id: 'who-bore-the-cost', title: 'Who bore the cost' },
  { id: 'a-different-response', title: 'A different response to harm' },
  { id: 'sources', title: 'Sources and limitations' },
];
---

<GovernanceDocument
  page="war-on-drugs"
  title="War on Drugs & Restorative Justice"
  category="Public education"
  description="Decades of punishment have not delivered the safety they promised. HTA supports harm reduction and accountability that takes harm seriously, gives people a choice, and looks for ways to repair it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="what-the-drug-war-cost" aria-labelledby="what-the-drug-war-cost-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="what-the-drug-war-cost-heading">What the drug war cost</h2>
    <p>We regard the War on Drugs as one of the most destructive policies in modern U.S. history.</p>
    <p>
      In June 1971, President Richard Nixon called drug abuse “public enemy number one” and announced an intensified
      federal campaign. <a href="#source-nixon" aria-label="Source 1: Richard Nixon’s remarks">[1]</a> It included treatment
      and prevention, but punitive enforcement became a defining part of the U.S. response. An Associated Press investigation
      estimated that the country had spent about $1 trillion on the effort by 2010, after roughly four decades. That is a
      historical estimate across several kinds of spending, not an audited total or a measure of what the United States spends
      today. <a href="#source-ap" aria-label="Source 2: Associated Press spending estimate">[2]</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      A Pew analysis of 2014 state data found no statistically significant association between higher drug imprisonment
      rates and lower self-reported drug use (excluding marijuana), drug arrest rates, or overdose death rates. That
      comparison cannot establish cause and effect. It does undermine the simple claim that more imprisonment reliably
      brings less harm. <a href="#source-pew" aria-label="Source 3: Pew’s state analysis">[3]</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The National Institute of Justice also finds that increasing the severity of punishment does little to deter
      crime. <a href="#source-nij" aria-label="Source 4: National Institute of Justice on deterrence">[4]</a> We believe
      public resources should prioritize practical ways to reduce harm: accurate education, overdose prevention, voluntary
      treatment, and peer support.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-bore-the-cost" aria-labelledby="who-bore-the-cost-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="who-bore-the-cost-heading">Who bore the cost</h2>
    <p>
      Enforcement has never landed evenly. The ACLU’s analysis of marijuana arrests from 2010 through 2018 found that
      Black people were 3.64 times as likely as white people to be arrested for marijuana possession, despite comparable
      use. That finding is specific to marijuana possession in the period studied; it shows how a policy can carry very
      different consequences for different communities. <a
        href="#source-aclu"
        aria-label="Source 5: ACLU analysis of marijuana arrests">[5]</a
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      Federal cocaine sentencing shows another disparity. Under the 1986 law, trafficking five grams of crack cocaine
      triggered the same five-year federal mandatory minimum as trafficking 500 grams of powder cocaine. The U.S.
      Sentencing Commission reported that 81.8% of defendants sentenced for federal crack cocaine offenses in fiscal
      year 2006 were Black. Those figures describe the rule and its effects at the time, not the law today.
      <a href="#source-ussc" aria-label="Source 6: U.S. Sentencing Commission cocaine sentencing report">[6]</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      An arrest or conviction can have consequences long after the case ends. Any honest conversation about drug policy
      has to weigh those consequences alongside the harms the policy is supposed to prevent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="a-different-response" aria-labelledby="a-different-response-heading">
    <h2 class="hta-section-title" id="a-different-response-heading">A different response to harm</h2>
    <p>
      Restorative justice asks what happened, who was affected, and what meaningful accountability or repair could look
      like. The people affected should have a real say. Participation must be voluntary, and people can withdraw. No one
      should be pressured to meet with someone, accept an apology, forgive, or give up other options. <a
        href="#source-unodc"
        aria-label="Source 7: United Nations on restorative justice">[7]</a
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      HTA’s community has used restorative approaches when people were willing to take part. They are not right for
      every situation. When participation is unsafe, unwanted, or ruled out by a serious power imbalance, safety and
      other accountability options come first. A restorative conversation is not a substitute for emergency help or for
      a fair, independent review of serious allegations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read our <a href={getPermalink('/code-of-conduct')}>Ethical Code of Conduct overview</a> for more on the difference
      between voluntary repair and independent review.
    </p>
  </section>

